<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened-runtime entitlements for Vocara.app (rust/packaging/macos/bundle.sh
  signs every bundle with this file; the release workflow re-signs the
  notarized one with it, the hardened runtime and the Developer ID).
  Deliberately as good as EMPTY: the hardened runtime applies in full. The
  one key is a resource the user grants, not a protection weakened:
    - com.apple.security.device.audio-input - the microphone (live
      monitoring, recording takes, analysis input;
      crates/vocara_services/src/mic_permission.rs): under the hardened
      runtime a process may not open the microphone without it, whatever the
      user answers. It only lets macOS ask them (with Info.plist's
      NSMicrophoneUsageDescription).

  Vocara is not App-Sandboxed, as of the first release after 0.3.0: like
  Clew, it updates itself by replacing its own bundle in /Applications, which
  a sandboxed app cannot do. So no sandbox entitlement is declared, and none
  of the keys that only mean something inside the sandbox either.

  What used to be here, and why it is gone:
    - com.apple.security.app-sandbox. Every release up to 0.3.0 was
      sandboxed and kept its data in its container
      (~/Library/Containers/<bundle id>); the first launch of a later one
      moves that library to ~/Library/Application Support/<bundle id>
      (crates/vocara_services/src/sandbox_migration.rs).
    - com.apple.security.network.client (the update check's outbound HTTP)
      and com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-only / .read-write
      (the open and save panels' imports and exports): sandbox permissions.
      An unsandboxed process reaches the network and the files the user
      picks without them.
  Nothing here was needed by the hardened runtime: the app JIT-compiles
  nothing in process (Metal compiles shaders in Apple's own service), maps
  no writable executable memory, and loads no third-party dylibs (one
  statically linked executable; the Python separation sidecar of dev builds
  is a separate process under its own signature).

  Re-adding an entitlement takes a reproduced failure under the hardened
  runtime, the narrowest key that fixes it, and a line here naming the
  component that needs it. Re-adding the sandbox would put every user's
  library back out of reach (it would resolve inside the container again).
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
